   "text": "The observed pattern fits PrFeO3 (mp-22341), a distorted orthorhombic perovskite, better than the other candidates. Peak positions were read by counting along the 0.05\u00b0 grid.\n\n- **Main peak:** the observed maximum is at about 32.15\u201332.2\u00b0. For PrFeO3 the expected strongest reflections cluster around 31.4\u201332.3\u00b0: (020) at about 31.4\u00b0, (112) at about 31.9\u00b0 and (200) at about 32.3\u00b0. There is also a small shoulder near 31.6\u00b0, which could be (020).\n- **Low-angle doublet:** a weak doublet sits at about 22.5\u201322.7\u00b0. This fits PrFeO3 (110)/(002), expected at about 22.4\u201322.5\u00b0.\n- **Weak peak near 25.2\u00b0:** this matches PrFeO3 (111), expected at about 25.1\u00b0.\n- **Further peaks** appear at about 45\u00b0, 56\u00b0 and 66\u00b0, consistent with the usual higher-angle peaks of an orthorhombic perovskite. These were not indexed peak by peak.\n- **CaHfO3 (main alternative):** its larger cell would put the strongest peak near 31.5\u00b0 and the doublet near 22.15\u00b0, both lower than observed.\n- **Cubic perovskites (BaScO3, BaSrZrSnO6):** their larger lattice parameters predict peaks near 21\u00b0 and 30\u00b0, which do not appear.\n\nThe main peak sits a little higher than the PrFeO3 estimate, so a small offset or overlapping reflections remain possible. No second phase is needed to account for the peaks examined.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-22341\"], \"confidence\": 0.72, \"note\": \"Peaks at ~22.6, 25.2, 32.2 and about 45 degrees match the smaller orthorhombic perovskite PrFeO3 better than CaHfO3, whose larger cell would shift peaks to lower angles.\"}",
